Revision history for Proc-Spawner (EXPERIMENTAL)

0.14  2026-11-27
      - Six records no longer ship a column that the tools make again from
        the corpora: what each setup did (large-subset-env-setups.tsv,
        -env-control.tsv, -env-affects-shell.tsv) and the snippet's text
        (large-subset-step3.tsv, -rejected.tsv, -rejected-envs.tsv); 2.50
        MB to 0.96 MB.  large-subset-restore.pl (new) restores them from
        corpus-work/samples.jsonl alone (make corpora corpus-samples; the
        setups remade by large-subset-blocks.pl -d and large-subset-
        setups.pl, the accepted samples taken from the provenance, so no
        scan; no snippet runs); each record's header gives the command.
        The writers (large-subset-matchreport.pl -record, large-subset-
        blocks.pl -p, large-subset-rejected.pl -o and -envs) leave the
        column out unless -full.  Checked: each record restored, from
        corpora fetched again and samples made again, equals the record as
        it was, byte for byte; the writers' reports are the same.  Step 3's
        setups read this system's files for absolute names (here /* matched
        only /container_info.json, in 2 rows of -env-affects-shell.tsv), so
        on another system those rows could come out otherwise: the two
        records' headers say so.  The six records stamped again (tools
        f54b18c); the document parts drawing on them marked in line (their
        counts are the same); doc/large-subset/README.md and
        large-subset-environment.md, section 12, say which columns are
        restored on demand.
      - The missing-settings matrix under Wine (Wine 9, Strawberry Perl
        5.38.2.2, the seven Windows test files), run on 0.14's code and
        still running at the release: 14 of 14 entries pass, the same 8
        settings skipped as missing already (doc/test-results/
        wine-missing-matrix.txt).
      - large-subset-builtins-differences.tsv lists only the builtin calls
        whose result differs beyond the messages, or that ShellSpawn alone
        rejects (174); the calls that differ in the messages only (24,600
        worded as dash's, in 26,541 samples; 1 as neither shell's) are
        counted in its header, not listed (large-subset-builtins.pl -l;
        each with its answers in the stored runs' builtins.jsonl).  The
        record went from 5.1 MB (24,776 lines, 38% of the unpacked
        distribution, there since 0.12, when A23 made every program not on
        the runner's list "not found") to 34 KB.  Made again from the
        stored runs of 2026-11-22 (-reuse): its 174 lines are byte for byte
        the old record's; stamped again (large-subset-tables-used.tsv).
        large-subset-state.md, section 7 (and its html), says so.
      - The work list's 34 candidates (the 9 that came out alike and the 25
        that differ, that no decision keeps out) in example environments
        written by reading them (record large-subset-rejected-setups.jsonl,
        kept by hand; make corpus-rejected-envs, new, with
        large-subset-rejected.pl -split and -envs; record
        large-subset-rejected-envs.tsv, stamped with the new collection
        2026-11-26-post-0.13-rejected-envs).  25 read something from their
        environment and ran in one to three environments: 5 alike in every
        one, 8 in some, 12 in none; 9 read nothing.  So 8 of the 9 alike
        remain candidates (ls ~- differs once OLDPWD is set); of the 25
        that differ, 7 come out alike in some environments.  README.md,
        large-subset-state.md section 4 (and its html), TODO A27.
      - README.md, the rejected snippets, cut from 1,218 words to 377: the
        groups, one table of the run in dash and bash, what the parser could
        accept and why none is ready; the whole account (every list, the
        reasons, the run) moved into large-subset-state.md, section 4, new
        subsection "The rejected samples, run in dash and bash".
      - TODO is TODO.md: each item keeps its plain text, in a code block
        after its own anchor (my-aN for part A's item N, my-part-a to
        my-part-d, my-b- and the first words for the others, my-f1); every
        reference to a TODO item in the .md documents is a link to it (69
        in 11 documents, none to an item that no longer exists).  MANIFEST,
        CLAUDE.md.
      - Proc-Spawner-results made ready to be public: names of the run
        machine's own files that snippets listing the whole file system had
        put into three collections (session identifiers, two private
        repositories' trees) replaced by placeholders, and its history
        replaced by one commit.  The collection 2026-11-22-post-0.13 has a
        new archive (SHA-256 a9983eb1...; the same members, metadata and
        order, packed in one xz block, so 7.6 MB, not 7.9): its row in
        large-subset-tables-used.tsv changed accordingly, and make
        corpus-rereport from it gave every record byte for byte.  X86
        (open): a new run would catch such names again.
      - README.md after the author's reading of the run of the rejected
        snippets: a prominent link to the repository Proc-Spawner-results
        (the records of every run); one term, "did not end", where the
        README said also endless, ran out of time and timeout (the group
        of the accepted snippets renamed so); "the parser could learn"
        said as what it means (the large parser accepts the form, and
        ShellSpawn runs it doing what both shells do, or what dash does,
        A5); every rejected snippet that came out alike needs runs in
        example environments, made by reading it, before the parser
        accepts it (TODO A27, rewritten).  large-subset-state.md, section
        1: the yardsticks are judged from the text, not from runs (30 of
        the 45 of the work list differ when run); section 4's "should
        learn" reworded.
      - README.md, the work list: why one run is not enough, with the
        example found by hand: ls ~- (one of the 9 that came out alike)
        differs as soon as OLDPWD is set (bash lists the directory, dash
        does not expand ~-); what example environments are, said plainly.
      - The 1,543 snippets the large parser rejects run in dash and bash
        alone (2026-11-26; make corpus-rejected, new: large-subset-
        rejected.pl prepares the run and compares dash with bash as the
        match compares; record large-subset-rejected.tsv, stamped with the
        new collection proc-spawner/2026-11-26-post-0.13-rejected of
        Proc-Spawner-results).  Work list 45: 15 alike (6 of them kept
        rejected by B1, B3, D1 and the set of other programs; 9 the parser
        could learn), 30 differ (19 both fail with different statuses, 6
        one fails, 5 other output).  Subtle 40: 20 alike, 20 differ.
        Foreign 1,458: 313 alike (123 of them refused by both), 1,138
        differ, 7 time out.  Corrected by it: README.md said the 12
        expansion errors of the work list fail alike in both shells
        (copied from large-subset-state.md, section 4): 9 fail with
        different statuses (dash 2, bash 127), bash 5.2 runs ${args[@]@Q}
        (2), ${1~~} stands in a function never called (1); section 4 also
        listed export ${foo?} among the forms the shells agree on (2/127).
        README.md (the rejected snippets), large-subset-state.md section 4
        (and its html), TODO A27 (what to do with them) and A3.
      - X85 fixed (known issues): the runner waited for ever for a run
        that stops its own process group (bash's suspend -f stops timeout
        with it); now killed after 15 s, status 124, "hung" in the record.
        Checked on suspend -f and BC:ch10/hard_to_kill; a run that ends by
        itself is not changed (timeout ends every shell within 6 s), so no
        record is made again (environment.md section 12 marked in line).
      - README.md: the level partially decided says which program finds
        the dependences (large-subset-blocks.pl) and what they are.
      - README.md, the rejected snippets, after a test by a reader without
        context: the yardsticks said to be the detector's (from the text,
        not runs); "alike" defined; a decision can keep out what the shells
        agree on; every foreign subgroup's outcome given (2 time-outs and
        the 2 of the 125 that differ had been left out); the 19 that both
        fail counted out plainly (10 expansion errors); of the 30 of the
        work list that differ, 5 are kept out by decisions (B3's three
        ulimit, the two set -o), so TODO A27 (b) asks about 25.
      - README.md, "Threads" rewritten after the author's reading: no longer
        "use the modules from one thread only", but what a start and a run
        change in Perl's process, when and for how long, so that a program
        with threads can keep the damage local.  A table per setting and
        approach (POSIX) with three marks: C made by the call (given to the
        primitive), A around the call (Perl's process changed and put
        back), R refused (start returns undef, spawn the empty list, $! =
        ENOTSUP; or dropped, per setting, with unsupported => 'drop' or
        %Proc::Spawner::UNSUPPORTED); notes for each mark that depends on
        the C library or the value; Windows in a paragraph; what
        ShellSpawn's starts ask for (umask, sigdefault, chdir) and what
        run() changes.  Taken from lib/Proc/Spawner.pm (spawn,
        spawn_windows, %MISSING's comment, the POD of spawn) and
        lib/Proc/ShellSpawn.pm (the start of a program, ignoring_int,
        open_end).  Approach 1 no longer said to make settings "in the
        child".
      - README.md, "Threads", after a test by a reader without context: the
        exceptions to C (note 2, Windows' handles) and to "every thread
        sees" (the mask and nice value per thread) named where C and A are
        defined; starts whose settings are all C may be made in several
        threads at once, the others and runs of snippets from one thread
        at a time; the list of what other threads should not use made
        complete (every A row); $PRIMITIVE and %MISSING to learn the
        approach at run time; %UNSUPPORTED's keys; Windows' closefrom,
        sigmask, sigdefault, resetids; not tested with threads.  The
        approaches part: why a new group cannot be made around the call.
      - README.md after the author's second reading: the ways of starting a
        child are called approaches, no longer levels (which also named
        the levels of agreement of the corpus snippets), in README.md,
        doc/README.md, pspawn-spawner.md, pspawn-known-issues.md (X64),
        t/missing-matrix.pl's comment, TODO (A26, F1); the anchor
        my-the-levels is my-the-approaches.  "Threads" says per approach,
        in a table for POSIX and a list for Windows, which settings of a
        child are made in the child and which around the call, and apart
        what running a snippet changes at every approach.  The date of the
        runs moved from the section's opening into the parts it holds for.
        Every "mostly" of README.md made a count, from the stored runs of
        2026-11-22 (runreport -c over corpus-work/run, the match, the
        threeway tables): the messages 23,135 of 23,391 not found / command
        not found; neither 150 = $0 41, process IDs 25, times 15, the
        interactive builtins 36, 33 others; the calls 66+14+13+12+7+2=114;
        dash's side 1,268 of 1,798 in the opening; the 20 skipped tests
        under Wine said not recorded by kind.  A new part, "Counts that do
        not match", with an apology: 2+1 against 4 (NB:9614, A4, differs
        only in an example environment), 1,221 against 1,798 (1,206 + 592;
        the 15 more are messages in output or files, which the match
        drops: checked snippet by snippet), 1,333 against 1,154 (not
        reconciled), messages in the flipped assumptions, Windows' 131;
        TODO, part B.  B1, B3, D1 said in words; "values passed on" two
        levels; the further measurements say what each shows.  TODO A27:
        whether to run the rejected snippets in dash and bash.
      - README.md after a test by a reader without context: "we" for
        ShellSpawn replaced by its name in How close (D4's checklist); the
        1,798 split by the shell ShellSpawn follows (1,268+355+128+47,
        match.txt), so that the 1,268 of the opening and the 1,248
        legalized are told apart; Threads: approaches 1 and 2 both make
        settings in the child, the exception of Windows' handles named;
