        CLAUDE.md.
      - Which runs made each record: doc/large-subset/tools/
        large-subset-tables-used.tsv names, for each of the 18 records of the
        corpus runs, the collection of Proc-Spawner-results it was made
        from (with its archive's SHA-256), the commit of the runs' code,
        the commit of the tools that wrote it, its own SHA-256, and the make
        target that writes it again.  make corpus-stamp writes it
        (large-subset-stamp.pl, whose %RECORD lists the records);
        t/records.t checks it in every make test (an installation's too;
        make docs-check runs it as well):
        the records always, the commits in a git clone, the collections
        with a clone of Proc-Spawner-results beside this one (or
        PROC_SPAWNER_RESULTS=DIR); a failure names the make target to run,
        and a level it cannot check is skipped with the reason (no git, a
        clone of another repository, a directory given that does not exist
        or is not such a clone, nothing beside it) and the command that
        would let it check: printed in a git clone of Proc-Spawner or with
        RELEASE_TESTING or PROC_SPAWNER_RESULTS set, elsewhere (an
        installation) only with prove -v.  About 0.15 s.  Its git commands
        are written for cmd.exe too (the null device of File::Spec, double
        quotes).  make corpus-rereport
        (large-subset-rereport.sh, from the collection's rereport.sh of
        2026-11-22): the reports and records of stored runs made again
        with this tree's tools, no shell run.  The first table: the runs
        of 2026-11-22 (collection 2026-11-22-post-0.13, code f781213).  The
        11 records the reports write were made again from that collection
        with make corpus-rereport (7 min 38 s): 10 byte for byte as on
        main, large-subset-endmodes.tsv with the same rows in another order
        (X83); the 5 records the runs write themselves (the three-way
        comparison's two, the three *-calls.jsonl) are as the run wrote
        them (f781213); the 2 lists read (-ai-classes, -match-known) as
        they were.
