          Against the table of post-0.09, 2 samples changed class:
          - `printf "\u20AC %.2f\n" 123.4` ("same" to "ShellSpawn differs"):
            the in-process `printf` printed `€` in the C locale, where
            coreutils prints `\u20AC`. This is **known issue X37** (our bug),
            fixed since: run again in the same run's conditions, ShellSpawn
            then prints `\u20AC 123.40`, as both shells do.
          - `{ echo foo; cat bar; } | tee bar` (a race, "ShellSpawn as dash"
            to "as bash").
          The external calls are the same as in the run of post-0.09 (its
          count is in "The atomic calls of external programs", below). Of the
          1,055, ShellSpawn's results changed in 5: X37, the race, and 3 that
          change between any two runs (process IDs, a pipe's inode number).
          The "coreutils involved" split is the logging run's: which samples
          hand such a call does not change, as the call goes inside Perl
          instead of to a program.
          W5: the event loop over the library (after 0.10; 2026-11-09), on
          Linux and under Wine
          Measured 2026-11-09, after W5 and its Windows follow-up (on Linux
          only its documentation changed), with X37 fixed.
          **On Linux.** ShellSpawn alone ran over the whole library (`-shells
          ShellSpawn -workers-from`, the same environment), compared with the table
          of the previous part sample by sample (`large-subset-runreport.pl
          -ours-from … -ours-vs …`). ShellSpawn changed in 55 = 52 + 2 + 1:
          - 52 also change between two runs of the same code (process IDs,
            times, `sort -R`, races);
          - 2 more by timing (whether `echo` dies of SIGPIPE first; `tee
            /dev/stderr` truncating the messages file before or after a
            message);
          - X37's sample, now as both shells.
          Joined with the run above, 17 samples changed class. All 17, run
          again with the three shells at once, came out as in the table above,
          but for timing in messages and one inode number (`ls -i`). The
          changes came from joining runs of different days (`ls -la` shows the
          date of `..`, the worker's directory, which `-env mtime` now fixes
          too) and from the load of the Wine measurement below. So W5 changes
          nothing over the library on Linux.
          **Under Wine** (Strawberry Perl 5.38.2.2, the DLL built from the
          same code). The library could not run there, as `Proc::ShellSpawn`
          was not yet ported to Windows (it was later that day:
          `../pspawn-shell-windows.md`; the library has not been run there
          since). Every `run()` died at once, first at `getppid`
          (unimplemented in Windows Perl; `$PPID`), then, past that in a
          scratch copy, at `fcntl` with `F_GETFL` in `Proc::Spawner::Graph`.
          What did run there:
          | What | Result |
          |---|---|
          | `t/loop.t` | 17 passed, 1 skipped (no console for Ctrl-C) |
          | `t/windows.t` | 14 passed |
          | `t/parsers.t` | 4 passed, 2 skipped (no dash or bash) |
          | `t/programs.t` | 3 passed, 1 skipped (no coreutils) |
          | the parser over every extracted sample | all 45,501 trees (or rejections) the same as on Linux |
