      - Review of the executor's code, the user's marks of 2026-11-15
        (decision page "Executor Idiom Review"):
        - JH1, JB1 (X60, ours): loops whose steps finish at once ran each
          next step from inside the one before, so 100 items or more (export
          a1 ... a100, a here-document or a quoted word with 100 expansions,
          a case with 100 patterns) made Perl warn "Deep recursion" and grow
          in memory.  One helper, loop_async, now runs every such loop
          (each_async, expand_list, a word's parts, redirections, if, case,
          settings, an in-process program's writes, jobs, hash).  Test:
          t/long-lists.t (new).
        - JB2 (X61, ours): read kept a final delimiter that dash and bash
          drop when IFS blanks stand before it (echo "a :" | IFS=" :" read
          x gave "a :", theirs "a").  Fixed; 7,500 random cases without
          backslashes now all agree.  Two lines in t/batteries/t1.txt;
          t/batteries/portable.txt recorded again (also 25 battery lines
          added since its last record).
        - JB3: the POD of Proc::ShellSpawn::Programs had an empty C<>
          ($Proc::ShellSpawn::ECHO, lost when it was written).
        - JH2: settings that stand alone with redirections (x=1 >f) go
          through with_redirs, as cd does, instead of repeating its steps;
          a simple command calls redirect once, and then sees whether any
          word is left.
        - JH5 (a): Proc::Spawner's two places that change this process's
          directory around the call (with posix_spawn or fork, and through
          Run) share dir_change and dir_back.  (b) not done: not marked.
        - JU1: a field under expansion is a hash (text, pat, keep, glob,
          gone), not an array by number; one comment lists the five.
        - JU2: :, exit, break with continue, and read have functions of
          their own (b_colon, b_exit, b_break, b_read), like the other
          builtins; Proc::ShellSpawn::builtin is gone.
        - JU4: the $(( )) parser is two named functions over variables set
          with local for the call, as test's parser is written; the weak
          references of E21 go (t/memory.t: no growth).  Each thread has its
          own interpreter, so its own copies of them.
        - JU5: the scalar $ENV of test's parser is $CMDENV.
      - X65 (ours; found under Wine): on Windows a shell of ours saw
        inherited variables under upper-case names only (Perl's %ENV
        there); export z=3; sh -c 'echo $z' printed nothing.  Fixed (A22
        (a), the user): the names as the environment block spells them
        (XSUB __win_env_names), PATH as PATH; PROC_SHELLSPAWN_ENVNAMES=upper
        for the old way (A22 (b)).
      - JU3: Large and Builtins call the shell layer through the alias
        ProcSS (set by Proc::ShellSpawn), not by imported short names; the
        longer lines stay unwrapped (the user).
      - Windows' priority classes elsewhere (the user, 2026-11-15):
        win_priority on POSIX is mapped to nice, the increment from ours to
        the class's nice value (idle 16, below_normal 8, normal 0,
        above_normal -8, high -16, realtime -20); nice, if given, wins; an
        unknown class fails with EINVAL (before: unsupported).  The values
        are Cygwin's (winprio_to_nice, nice_to_winprio), and W4's mapping
        of nice to a class on Windows now uses Cygwin's ranges too (12 and
        up idle, 4 to 11 below_normal, -4 to 3 normal, -5 to -12
        above_normal, -13 and below high; before: our own table).
      - At exit, as the shells (K5, the user, 2026-11-15: "as shell"):
        Proc::ShellSpawn's END no longer waits for every background job to
        end.  The shells leave their jobs running; ShellSpawn starts each command
        of a job itself (no fork), so it waits only while it has something
        left to do for a job (a command still to start, a read or write of
        its own, a call queued), then exits and leaves the running processes
        alone.  So `tail -F x | tee -a y &' (NB:9731), which kept Perl
        from exiting (X1), no longer does; `(sleep 1; echo x) &' still runs
        its echo, 1 s after the shells would have exited.
        PROC_SHELLSPAWN_END, or $Proc::ShellSpawn::END_WAIT at run time
        (a stopgap: such choices are to become arguments of the API):
        MODE[:SECONDS], MODE shell (the default), all (wait for every job:
        as before), none (exit at once: a job's commands not yet started
        never start); SECONDS: wait at most that long.  Proc::Spawner's
        loop: wait_until(CODE, TIMEOUT), true if CODE came true; queued().
        t/at-exit.t (11 tests).  The runner: -end MODE (ShellSpawn's mode, hidden
        from the samples).
      - large-subset-repeat.pl (make corpus-races): where ShellSpawn varies, the
        pairs of a sample and an environment are split by whether each
        shell repeated, only one, or neither (the user, 2026-11-15): only
        where a shell repeats is it a difference of ours; where both vary
        there is no one behaviour to compare with.  Reported as each + one
        (the run without waiting: 3 + 8 = 11 pairs, 5 samples), and per
        environment when there are several.
