      - W7 (the user, 2026-11-14): on Windows new_group is 'auto' by
        default: a child gets a process group of its own (where Windows
        turns Ctrl-C off) only if it would have SIGINT ignored on POSIX
        (sigignore INT, or ignored here and not in sigdefault: the shell
        layer's background jobs); every other child stays in Perl's group
        and gets a Ctrl-C at the console itself, as Perl's own system does
        (a new group only for system 1, ...).  1 and 0: always, never.
        The POD of %WINDOWS lists what the defaults cost (children that
        outlive Perl, Ctrl-C only on this console, Perl's kill TERM and a
        child in Perl's group, system1).  Ctrl-Break, which no setting
        changes, is a remark after that list, not one of the costs.  Tested under Wine by
        the flag Windows records for a new group (t/win_spawn.t, both
        primitives); not with a real Ctrl-C (TODO B).
      - W6 (the user, 2026-11-14): on Windows kill_with_parent is off by
        default (%Proc::Spawner::WINDOWS; win_spawn.h's WIN_OPT_DEFAULT
        too, so Perl's copy inherits none of it), as with Perl's own
        spawn: a child still running when Perl ends keeps running.  The
        POD of %WINDOWS, documented at last, says what that costs (after a
        Ctrl-C, with new_group on, every child goes on; files a child keeps
        open; the console) and what the option costs when on.  Tests:
        t/windows.t (a child outlives its parent by default, ends with it
        with the option on), t/win_spawn.t (no job by default).
